WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.232 --> 00:00:01.065
- Hostile?

00:00:01.065 --> 00:00:02.495
I show you hostile.

00:00:02.495 --> 00:00:05.070
(punching)
(grunting)

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.370
So funny story, that actually
happened to me in court

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.613
and I also punched the witness.

00:00:09.613 --> 00:00:12.363
(cheerful music)

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:17.150
Hey Legal Eagles, D. James
Stone here teaching you how

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:20.380
to think like a lawyer so
you can crush law school.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.610
You know, growing up I
loved watching legal movies.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.500
There's nothing better
than seeing great lawyers

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.220
stick it to evil corporations
or obstreperous witnesses.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.260
It's one of the reasons
that I went to law school

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.530
and became a trial lawyer myself.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.230
And now that I've been an attorney

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.420
at a major law firm for over 10 years,

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:38.950
I thought I'd take some time and review

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.570
some of the most famous legal
scenes from movie history.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:43.970
So let's dive right in.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:46.520
The first scene comes from
one of the most famous

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:50.163
courtroom scenes of all
time in A Few Good Men.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.030
- Defense will be calling Airman

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.420
Cecil O'Malley and
Airman Anthony Rodriguez.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.620
They were working the ground crew at

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.180
Andrews at 2 a.m. on the 7th.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.900
- Your Honor, these men
weren't on the list.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.140
- Rebuttal witnesses, Your
Honor, are called specifically

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.564
to refute testimony offered
under direct examination.

00:01:06.564 --> 00:01:09.514
- So this is actually fairly accurate.

00:01:09.514 --> 00:01:12.260
One of the things that
people don't know about trial

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.740
is that there's so much you have to do

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.900
before you go into the courtroom.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.870
It takes weeks and
sometimes months to prepare

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:23.810
your pretrial filings and one
of the things that you have

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.330
to do is you have to tell
the other side what evidence

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:30.020
you're going to present and
what witnesses you're going

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.480
to call and what Kevin Bacon is doing here

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.620
is he's saying that
these witnesses were not

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.000
on the disclosures from the defense

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.720
and therefore they shouldn't be allowed.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.240
And in normal circumstances,
that would be the case.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.690
If they weren't disclosed then the judge

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.530
would be correct in
disallowing those witnesses.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.940
However, there's an
exception to this which is

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.130
where you are calling witnesses

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.240
that are specifically
to rebut the evidence

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.940
that was presented by the other side.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:01.440
You effectively didn't know
that you had to call these

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.400
witnesses and they're only
there to rebut the evidence.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.540
So this is actually a part of trial law

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.960
that you don't really
see in movies very much,

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.260
hearkening back to all of
those pretrial filings.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.083
So this is actually fairly accurate.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:16.820
- I'll allow the witnesses.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.470
- This is ridiculous.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:19.670
- Colonel, a moment ago--

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:22.820
- Check the tower logs, for Christ's sake.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.040
- I would not expect a witness to

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.710
opine from the stand like that.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:29.620
They usually are only allowed to speak

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.805
when the question is presented to them.

00:02:31.805 --> 00:02:35.860
And Tom Cruise would have been
within his rights to object

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.510
and to move to strike the
statement as nonresponsive.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.213
- Well we'll get to the
airman in just a minute, sir.

00:02:43.449 --> 00:02:45.470
A moment ago you said that you
ordered Lieutenant Kendrick

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:49.360
to tell his men that Santiago
wasn't to be touched.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:50.890
- That's right.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:54.160
- And Lieutenant Kendrick
was clear on what you wanted?

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:54.993
- Crystal.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.430
- So, hard for a witness to know

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.760
what another person was thinking.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.823
He's opining about the mental
state of another person.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.450
That's probably speculation and probably

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:12.543
wouldn't be allowed in most courts.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:17.290
- Any chance Lieutenant
Kendrick ignored the order?

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:18.550
- Ignored the order?

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:20.750
- [Daniel] Any chance he forgot about it?

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:21.640
- No.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.520
- Any chance Lieutenant
Kendrick left your office

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.243
and said the old man is wrong?

00:03:26.243 --> 00:03:27.750
- No.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.330
- When Lieutenant Kendrick spoke to you--

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.800
- At this point, there's
no foundation being laid

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.210
as to how the Colonel would
know any of this information.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.180
It's possible that he would,
but you're not allowed

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.870
to just go up there and ask someone

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.710
to talk about things that other people did

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.770
without laying the proper foundation

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:48.850
that they have the personal knowledge

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:50.770
to know what actually happened.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.822
You can't just say I gave an order

00:03:52.822 --> 00:03:57.050
and I know everyone is always
going to follow that order.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:58.920
Although I think that
that's sort of the point,

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.250
the trap that Tom Cruise is laying here.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.570
- When Lieutenant Kendrick
spoke to the platoon

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.310
and ordered them not to touch Santiago,

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:07.710
any chance they ignored him?

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.550
- You ever served in
an infantry unit, son?

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:13.383
- No, sir.

00:04:13.383 --> 00:04:17.050
- So generally, witnesses are
not allowed to ask questions

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:19.050
of the attorney when they're
on the witness stand.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.830
The attorney is the only one that's

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:22.540
allowed to ask the questions here

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.320
and Tom Cruise would be well within

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.470
his rights to ask the judge to instruct

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:27.950
the witness to answer the question.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:29.660
He's being nonresponsive.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.050
But at the same time,
he's employing a tried

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:37.180
and true strategy of
giving the witness rope

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.272
so that they can hang themselves.

00:04:39.272 --> 00:04:42.540
Here we know that Jack
Nicholson is going to

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.810
make some crucial
admissions in just a second.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:48.040
And by giving him the
freedom to do these things

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.730
that you wouldn't normally
do on the witness stand,

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.693
he's falling into the trap
that Tom Cruise has laid here.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:56.210
- Yes, sir.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:58.570
- Are we clear?
- Are we clear?

00:04:58.570 --> 00:04:59.963
- Crystal.
- Crystal.

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:03.630
- Colonel, I have just one more question

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.393
before I call Airman O'Malley
and Airman Rodriguez.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:12.470
If you gave an order that
Santiago wasn't to be touched

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.270
and your orders are always followed,

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:19.363
then why would Santiago be in danger?

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:23.440
- So technically this is
an argumentative question.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.070
The argument is integral
to the question itself.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:28.702
It's one of those questions that's like

00:05:28.702 --> 00:05:31.070
when did you stop beating your wife?

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:33.340
Well that implies the argument that

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.150
you were in fact beating your wife.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.440
So here it's the same sort of thing

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.227
where he's laid this trap
and created an argument

00:05:41.227 --> 00:05:43.691
through the question
itself that Jack Nicholson

00:05:43.691 --> 00:05:46.160
can't really answer the question without

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.360
falling into the argument
that's being made.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.010
So it's not technically a proper question.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.470
But by the same token, he's simply using

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.110
the witness's words against them.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:56.293
And it's some nice legal trickery.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.647
- Why would it be necessary
to transfer him off the base?

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.820
- Santiago was a substandard Marine.

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:12.170
He was being transferred--
- That's not what you said!

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:13.730
You said he was being transferred

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:15.350
because he was in grave danger.

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:16.183
- [Nathan] That's correct.

00:06:16.183 --> 00:06:17.990
- You said he was in
danger, I said grave danger.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:20.033
You said is there any other--
- I recall what I said.

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:21.540
- I could have the court
reporter read back to you--

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:22.580
- I know what I said.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.610
I don't have to have it
read back to me like I'm--

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:26.303
- Then why do two orders?

00:06:26.303 --> 00:06:28.260
- So a couple of things going on here.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:31.190
Number one, you're not allowed
to interrupt the witness.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:33.040
Nor is the witness generally

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.230
allowed to interrupt the attorney.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:37.830
That is sort of the definition
of badgering the witness

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:41.660
and it's also muddying
up the record as well.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:44.790
Tom Cruise alludes to the stenographer

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:48.270
who is typing away every
word that's being said.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:52.060
And that's generally standard
practice in almost any trial.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:55.210
And the attorneys are allowed to read back

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:56.930
portions of the record to the witness

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:59.922
to get their clarifying statement.

00:06:59.922 --> 00:07:02.633
However, the judge is not going to allow

00:07:02.633 --> 00:07:05.050
the attorney and the witness
to talk over each other

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.500
because it's impossible
for the stenographer

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.670
to record two voices at the same time

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:11.620
and that simply muddies the record,

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:13.330
which is the last thing you want

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:15.710
because you have to have a pristine record

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:18.913
in order to take an appeal from the trial.

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:22.143
- Colonel.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:27.370
- Sometimes men take matters
into their own hands.

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:28.670
- No sir, you made it
clear just a moment ago

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:30.380
that your men never take
matters into their own hands.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.260
Your men follow orders or people die.

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:33.360
So Santiago shouldn't have been

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:34.870
in any danger at all,
should he have, Colonel?

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:37.460
- That's more argument.
- You snotty little bastard.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:39.240
- [Ross] Your honor, I'd
like to ask for a recess.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:40.650
- I'd like an answer
to the question, judge.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:42.503
- The court will wait for an answer.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:45.640
- [Daniel] If Lieutenant Kendrick--

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:48.290
- Which is funny, because
he asks for an answer

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:51.390
to the question he just
asked and then proceeds

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:53.280
to immediately ask another question.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.770
If you have a good question
and you need an answer to it,

00:07:56.770 --> 00:08:00.160
you are allowed to petition the judge to

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:01.950
instruct the witness to answer,

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:06.350
but you shouldn't then
immediately ask another question.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:08.240
Lawyers actually do this all the time.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.490
They forget the sequencing
of the questions.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:13.030
This happens both in
court and in depositions.

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:17.690
But it's not a good way
to lay a proper record.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:19.230
- Lieutenant Kendrick ordered
the code red, didn't he?

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:21.370
Because that's what you told
Lieutenant Kendrick to do.

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.036
- [Ross] Object!
- And when it went bad,

00:08:23.036 --> 00:08:24.981
you cut these guys loose!
- Your honor!

00:08:24.981 --> 00:08:26.227
- Kaffee, I'll hold you in contempt.

00:08:26.227 --> 00:08:27.551
- You had Markinson sign
a phony transfer order,

00:08:27.551 --> 00:08:29.600
and you doctored the log book!
- Your honor! Damn it, Kaffee!

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:30.630
- You coerced the doctor,
now I'm asking you--

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:31.670
- [Judge] Consider yourself in contempt.

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:35.020
- So you are not allowed to yell in court.

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:36.890
That is completely improper.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:40.420
Also, it ruins your image
in front of the jury.

00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:42.880
Credibility is everything
in front of the jury.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:45.450
And if you are this hothead
who's yelling in court,

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:48.400
you are going to ruin your
rapport with the jury.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.890
And so you're not going to
see a lot of yelling in court

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:54.880
in the way that Tom Cruise and
Kevin Bacon are doing here.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:56.920
- Son, we live in a world that has walls

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:59.560
and those walls have to be
guarded by men with guns.

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:00.510
Who's gonna do it?

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:01.440
You?

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.644
You, Lieutenant Weinberg?

00:09:03.644 --> 00:09:05.910
I have a greater responsibility

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:08.090
than you can possibly fathom.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:11.600
You weep for Santiago and
you curse the Marines.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.070
You have that luxury.

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:15.560
You have the luxury of
not knowing what I know,

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:19.690
that Santiago's death, while
tragic, probably saved lives.

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:20.810
And my existence--

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:25.810
- So it's a great line and
man, it makes for a good movie.

00:09:26.151 --> 00:09:28.630
But you're never going to see these

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:31.290
kind of Perry Mason moments in court.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:32.630
By the time you get to court,

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:34.240
you've been preparing for weeks.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:36.750
Your attorney has gone
over every conceivable

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:38.670
question that you are going to be asked.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:43.470
And they are going to help you
craft the testimony itself.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.730
And so trials are won and lost

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:49.030
on the evidence and the documents.

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:53.240
They are not won and lost
on assuming that the witness

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:56.410
is going to go nuts on the
stand and go into a shouting

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:59.680
match where they basically admit guilt.

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:01.120
That's not going to happen.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:05.390
- I don't give a damn what
you think you are entitled to.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:06.750
- Did you order the code red?

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:07.943
- [Nathan] I did the job--

00:10:07.943 --> 00:10:09.061
- Did you order the code red?

00:10:09.061 --> 00:10:10.583
- You're goddamn right I did!

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:18.470
- I have never once seen a
witness admit to the crime or the

00:10:19.205 --> 00:10:22.813
civil act that they are accused
of on the witness stand.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:24.893
Just doesn't happen.

00:10:24.893 --> 00:10:28.650
Somehow, witnesses know that
their ass is on the line

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:32.460
and they're not willing
to admit to something

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:33.450
that's against their interests.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:35.980
It just doesn't happen very often.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:37.220
The next scene comes from the

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:40.793
classic legal movie 12 Angry Men.

00:10:41.813 --> 00:10:43.650
- What actually happened is this.

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:46.960
The boy stayed home, had
another fight with his father,

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:48.210
stabbed him to death,

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:50.932
and left the house at 10 minutes after 12.

00:10:50.932 --> 00:10:53.600
He even remembered to wipe the
knife clean of fingerprints.

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:56.250
Now are you trying tell me that this knife

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.320
really fell through a
hole in the boy's pocket,

00:10:58.320 --> 00:10:59.640
someone picked it up off the street,

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:01.700
went to the boy's house,
and stabbed his father

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:03.650
with it just to test its sharpness?

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:05.760
- No, I'm just saying it's
possible the boy lost his knife

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:06.920
and that somebody else stabbed his

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:08.010
father with a similar knife.

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:09.890
It's just possible.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:11.840
- This is a really
interesting conversation

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:14.830
amongst jurors in the jury room

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:17.400
after the trial has concluded.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:22.400
A criminal trial has a very,
very high burden of proof.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:25.080
You have to prove beyond
a reasonable doubt.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:28.270
And you'll never get any judge to tell you

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:30.460
what mathematically that's supposed to be.

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:33.140
But a lawyer's shorthand would be that

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:38.140
beyond a reasonable doubt
has to be between 95 and 99%

00:11:38.865 --> 00:11:42.060
sure before you're allowed
to convict someone.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:44.300
As opposed to the civil arena,

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:46.200
which is where I have practiced,

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.340
where you only have to be 51% sure.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:54.190
You have to show your case
is true more likely than not.

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:57.793
So that's essentially 51% proven.

00:11:57.793 --> 00:12:01.760
But a criminal trial has a
much higher burden of proof.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:06.760
So this jury is debating
this very high level.

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:10.560
Is a coincidence enough
to show reasonable doubt?

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:12.200
And that's an open question

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:15.210
and it's the question that
they should be debating.

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:16.610
- Take a look at this knife.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:21.010
It's a very unusual knife.

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:22.640
I've never seen one like it.

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:25.510
Neither had the storekeeper
who sold it to the boy.

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:26.790
Aren't you asking us to accept

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:28.690
a pretty incredible coincidence?

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:30.930
- I'm just saying a
coincidence is possible.

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:32.733
- And I say it's not possible.

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:41.099
- Where did that come from?
(many men talking)

00:12:41.099 --> 00:12:42.383
- Ooh, another knife that
looks exactly the same.

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:45.670
- Where did you get it?

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:47.930
- I went out walking for a
couple of hours last night.

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:50.080
I walked through the boy's neighborhood.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:51.390
I bought that in a little pawn shop

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:53.300
just two blocks from the boy's house.

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:54.133
It cost $6.

00:12:55.429 --> 00:12:58.850
- So the jury is doing the right thing

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:01.090
by debating whether the prosecution

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:03.080
has proved their case
by a reasonable doubt.

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:06.680
However, a jury is not
allowed to bring in additional

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:09.940
evidence outside of what
was presented to them

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:13.040
by the prosecution and
told to them by the judge.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:14.893
That's completely improper.

00:13:14.893 --> 00:13:18.062
This jury would probably be disbanded

00:13:18.062 --> 00:13:22.150
and a mistrial declared for this kind of

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:26.583
act that goes beyond what
a jury is allowed to do.

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:30.450
- It's against the law to buy
ourselves switchblade knives.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:32.030
- That's right, I broke the law.

00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:33.840
- And so you pulled a real bright trick.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.970
Now I suppose you'll
tell me what it proves.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:38.440
- However, the defendant in this case

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:40.190
would have been well served if his lawyers

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:42.170
had done what Peter Fonda does here

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:44.790
and actually gone out to see where someone

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:46.410
could buy this really unusual knife.

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:48.710
Because it stands to reason that

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:51.700
if it is a completely unique knife,

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:54.470
then that is very probative of the idea

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:56.160
that he committed this murder.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.420
However, if it's a very common knife,

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.560
these jurors don't know
whether it's common or not,

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:03.620
so they should have been
presented with evidence

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:06.290
in the trial as to whether
the knife was common or not.

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:07.690
But they should not have brought

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:09.590
the knife into the jury room.

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:11.770
- Maybe there are 10
knives like that, so what?

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:13.993
- Maybe there are.
- Well what does it mean?

00:14:13.993 --> 00:14:16.070
You found another knife like it.

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:18.120
What's that, the discovery
of the age or something?

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:19.260
- You mean you're asking us to believe

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:20.470
that somebody else did the stabbing

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:22.340
with exactly the same kind of knife.

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:23.820
- The odds are a million to one.

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:26.660
- It's possible.
- But not very probable.

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:29.560
- That is a classic distinction
in criminal law between

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:34.210
something that is impossible
or probable or coincidental.

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:36.682
You'll often hear on TV and movies

00:14:36.682 --> 00:14:38.850
that the evidence is circumstantial.

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:42.390
Well the evidence is almost
always circumstantial.

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:44.960
That's not a criticism of the evidence

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:46.610
and it doesn't mean that circumstantial

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:48.940
evidence can't be incredibly strong.

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:51.750
The circumstantial evidence
here is probably pretty strong.

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:54.797
It's unlikely that another murderer

00:14:54.797 --> 00:14:58.042
used a similar or exactly the same knife.

00:14:58.042 --> 00:15:02.020
Direct evidence is the opposite
of circumstantial evidence,

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:05.150
which would be someone actually watching

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:08.180
the event taking place, for example.

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:10.760
Circumstantial evidence
can be very strong.

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:13.005
Just because someone says the
evidence is circumstantial

00:15:13.005 --> 00:15:16.700
doesn't mean that it's not good evidence.

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:19.110
And that's what the jury is debating here.

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:20.640
- You know what's interesting?

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:23.150
That he'd find a knife exactly
like the one the boy bought.

00:15:23.150 --> 00:15:25.120
- What's interesting about it?

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:26.150
Interesting.

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:28.033
- Well I don't know, I just
thought it was interesting.

00:15:28.033 --> 00:15:30.330
- There are still 11 of us
here who think he's guilty.

00:15:30.330 --> 00:15:31.500
- [Juror] Right, what do you think

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:32.333
you're going to accomplish?

00:15:32.333 --> 00:15:34.020
You're not going to change anybody's mind.

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:36.600
So if you want to be stubborn
and hang this jury, go ahead.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.340
The kid will be tried
again and found guilty.

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:39.513
Sure as he's born.

00:15:41.690 --> 00:15:42.780
- He's probably right.

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:44.690
- Okay, the next video comes from

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:47.793
the classic legal movie,
And Justice for All.

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:51.100
- And ladies and gentlemen of the jury,

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:58.250
the prosecution is not
gonna get that man today.

