So before you come back here
with another lame ass offer,

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:34.680
I want you to think real hard about

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:38.230
what your spine is worth,
Mr. Walker, or what you might

00:24:38.230 --> 00:24:41.480
expect someone to pay you
for your uterus, Ms. Sanchez.

00:24:41.480 --> 00:24:42.980
Then you take out your calculator

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:45.570
and you multiply that number by a hundred.

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:48.623
Anything less than that
is a waste of our time.

00:24:50.105 --> 00:24:54.600
- So she is hinting at one
of the crucial elements

00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:57.702
of a civil lawsuit, which
is that it is supposed to

00:24:57.702 --> 00:25:02.520
provide compensation for injury
that you actually incurred.

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:06.030
And in this particular case, there was,

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:09.740
it's actually based on a
real case in California

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:12.450
where a community's water was poisoned

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:14.810
and as a result they
had very high incidences

00:25:14.810 --> 00:25:18.950
of cancer and other deleterious ailments.

00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:23.040
And so if you tried every
individual one of these cases,

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:27.600
each individual might get
hundreds of thousands of dollars,

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:30.510
if not millions of dollars, from a party

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:32.870
if they were responsible for it.

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:36.100
But because it's a class action,
they're all lumped together

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:38.370
and Julia Roberts is saying, basically,

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:41.390
you need to compensate every
single one of these people

00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:43.520
for the injuries that they incurred

00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:47.083
and not just provide what
seems like a large number

00:25:47.083 --> 00:25:49.790
in the aggregate when
you have to spread it out

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:51.560
amongst a lot of different people.

00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:53.330
So it's a good negotiating tactic.

00:25:53.330 --> 00:25:58.200
It's a good way to anchor your position.

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:00.873
And it's pretty common
amongst class actions.

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:04.390
- By the way, we had that water

00:26:04.390 --> 00:26:06.870
brought in special for you folks.

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:08.420
It came from a well in Hinkley.

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:10.550
- That's hilarious.

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:27.080
- I think this meeting is over.

00:26:27.080 --> 00:26:28.193
- Damn right it is.

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:35.470
- So that's a fairly accurate depiction

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:40.010
of what happens when you have
two sides negotiating amongst

00:26:40.010 --> 00:26:42.700
each other, when you have
just the parties themselves.

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:45.120
It gets heated very often.

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:48.890
And that's why it's so common
to have a neutral third party,

00:26:48.890 --> 00:26:52.340
a mediator come in and
mediate the two sides.

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:54.130
I've used a mediator many times.

00:26:54.130 --> 00:26:56.040
It really helps advance the ball.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:59.237
The mediator can't force
anyone to do anything,

00:26:59.237 --> 00:27:03.325
but they can provide
some external validation

00:27:03.325 --> 00:27:06.242
and they can move things along.

00:27:06.242 --> 00:27:09.770
It's really money well spent
to make sure that you don't

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:12.793
reach an impasse in a negotiation
like what happens here.

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:17.350
- Good morning, good morning.
- Good morning.

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:20.140
- Ed, Erin, what's this?

00:27:20.140 --> 00:27:22.232
- May I?
- Yep, go ahead.

00:27:22.232 --> 00:27:25.460
- Well you know what, Mr. Potter?

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:28.210
We completely forgot
your birthday this year

00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:31.090
and seeing as how you've
been so good to me,

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:32.640
it seemed a terrible oversight.

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:34.480
So what Ed and I have been
doing the last few days

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:37.050
is putting together a present for you.

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:38.283
634.

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:42.250
They're all signed, every single one.

00:27:42.250 --> 00:27:43.810
- Holy shit.

00:27:43.810 --> 00:27:45.693
- Now don't go getting jealous, Theresa.

00:27:46.600 --> 00:27:48.100
We have something for you too.

00:27:49.370 --> 00:27:51.733
- This is actually a really
interesting part of the law

00:27:51.733 --> 00:27:56.050
that you almost never see
in movies and television.

00:27:56.050 --> 00:27:59.180
Erin Brockovich involves
a class action lawsuit,

00:27:59.180 --> 00:28:01.190
one that actually took place.

00:28:01.190 --> 00:28:05.740
And part of a class action
lawsuit is the idea that you have

00:28:07.415 --> 00:28:09.730
a small subsection of the total number

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:11.460
of people that were harmed called

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:13.420
representatives and they represent

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:16.880
a class of people that have been harmed.

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:20.000
And so you say that these
people are representative

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:24.100
of the entire class and
therefore whatever compensation

00:28:24.100 --> 00:28:27.370
the representatives get should
be given to the entire class.

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:30.533
And what Erin Brockovich
has done is she's gone out

00:28:30.533 --> 00:28:33.150
and she's gotten declarations from

00:28:33.150 --> 00:28:36.550
600 people saying that they were harmed.

00:28:36.550 --> 00:28:39.070
And so effectively they have 600

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:41.020
people representing the class.

00:28:41.020 --> 00:28:43.800
And that's important
because defense attorneys

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:47.990
try to defeat a class by showing that

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:51.160
class representatives
don't represent the class,

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:54.950
or their claims are unusual
or there are a whole number

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:59.120
of reasons why you would
defeat a class action

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:03.580
by disqualifying one or two class members.

00:29:03.580 --> 00:29:05.530
And often class actions only have

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:08.250
a single class member
or maybe two or three.

00:29:08.250 --> 00:29:11.390
So Erin Brockovich has gone
out and she's gotten over 600

00:29:12.560 --> 00:29:15.170
people to say that they want
to be members of the class,

00:29:15.170 --> 00:29:18.230
which really strengthens the class action

00:29:18.230 --> 00:29:20.010
lawsuit that they're trying to bring.

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:21.430
So you don't hear about that part

00:29:21.430 --> 00:29:22.720
of class actions very often,

00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:25.810
but that is pretty accurate
to what plaintiffs'

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:29.030
attorneys try and do in
the class action context.

00:29:29.030 --> 00:29:30.293
